# **MVA - Multivariable analysis**

# Case study: HIV Sentinel System in Spain - Poisson regression {.unnumbered}

## Objectives of this case study

At the end of the case study, participants should be able to:

-   perform Poisson regression and interpret the results,
-   identify overdispersion and use appropriate models to address it,
-   perform negative binomial regression and interpret the model

## Previous level of expertise assumed

Participants are expected to be familiar with data import, management as well as
descriptive and linear/logistic regression in R.

## Preparation for the case study

1.  Extract the contents of the folder named *poisson_mva* in the local
    laptop

2.  Create an Rstudio project in the folder *poisson_mva*. If you are
    unsure on how to do that, read the EpiRhandbook Chapter on [R
    projects](https://epirhandbook.com/en/new_pages/r_projects.html)

3.  Inside the folder *poisson_mva*, the subfolder "data" contains the data
    needed for this case study: *HIV_Spain.dta*. There is another file
    named *data2005.txt* which will be used for some optional tasks at
    the end.

4.  Subfolder scripts should be used to save any scripts related to the
    analysis. Inside "backup" you will find a solution script with the
    code of the case study named *poisson_analysis_backup.R*.

5.  Subfolder "outputs" could be used to store all outputs (tables,
    graphs, documents) that are the result of the analysis.

### **Scenario, study design and analysis plan**

### Introduction

In Spain, HIV infection is monitored through a sentinel network of
eleven HIV clinics specialising in counselling and HIV testing. These
clinics have been operating without major changes since the beginning of
the 90s, and epidemiological information and results of HIV tests have
been collected in a standardised
[way](https://www.eurosurveillance.org/content/10.2807/esm.09.05.00466-en)

This case study uses information for 1989–2004 on a) people who
seroconverted (persons with a recorded HIV-negative test followed by a
positive one) b) people who did not seroconvert (had one or more
negative test, never testing positive).

Even though people may have been tested several times, information only
on the first negative HIV test, the last negative HIV test and the first
positive HIV test (where applicable) are shown in the database.

Seroconverters are defined as persons with any negative test followed by
a positive one (for them, the variable *seroco* is equal to 1 ). CD4
counts (*cd4*) at the time of the first positive test were also recorded.

Healthcare workers collect epidemiological information (self-reported
risk behaviours including injecting drug use, condomless sex between
men, sex work and condomless heterosexual sex) from participants using a
brief questionnaire before an HIV test is performed.

Each participant can report more than one risk category, which is
subsequently recoded in separate variables: person who injects drugs
(*idu*), man who has condomless sex with men (*msm*), sexworker (*sexwork*)
and person with condomless heterosexual contacts (*hete*).

### Your task

You are one of the epidemiologists in charge of the Spanish sentinel
surveillance system and you have been asked to study and model the
number of HIV infections within this system.

