### **Step 3: Univariate analysis**

So, we have information on `r nrow(hiv.data)` people, the majority of whom (`r adorn_pct_formatting(tabyl(hiv.data, sex))[[2,3]]`) were male, with a mean age (at date of last test) of 31.1 years and a median age of `r median(hiv.data$age)` years (Q1=`r round(summary(hiv.data$age)[[2]], digits = 0)`, Q3=`r round(summary(hiv.data$age)[[5]])`). Information on transmission categories was available for all participants: `r adorn_pct_formatting(tabyl(hiv.data, hete))[[2,3]]` had condomless heterosexual sex, `r adorn_pct_formatting(tabyl(hiv.data, msm))[[2,3]]` were men who had condomless sexual contact with other men and almost `r adorn_pct_formatting(tabyl(hiv.data, idu))[[2,3]]` were injecting drug users. The majority of HIV tests (`r adorn_pct_formatting(tabyl(hiv.data$lasttestYgr))[[4,3]]`) were done between 2001 and 2004 at the centres in Madrid ( `r adorn_pct_formatting(tabyl(hiv.data$centro), digits = 0)[[1,3]]` ). Among those attending, `r fmt_count(hiv.data, seroco == "yes")` seroconverted. The mean CD4 count was `r round(mean(hiv.data$cd4, na.rm = T), digits = 0)` cells per mm³ (recall that a CD4 count <350 cells per mm³ within 91 days of HIV diagnosis indicates a late diagnosis).

You may wish to discuss whether an HIV seroconversion incidence of `r adorn_pct_formatting(tabyl(hiv.data, seroco))[[2,3]]` is high or not. In Spain, the estimated incidence of newly diagnosed HIV is 79.3 per one million inhabitants in 2009. Recall that here we focus on new infections in very specific groups at risk.


