### **Step 4: Poisson regression**

To this point, you have already compared the risks of seroconversion for different exposures and assessed which groups within the population have the highest risk of seroconversion.

By now, you may have realised that sex, age, the different modes of transmission, the year when the test was performed, and the centre region are statistically associated with HIV seroconversion. Through stratified analysis, it can be concluded that some of these variables have an impact on the estimate of the others. In order to investigate several risk factors simultaneously and assess for confounding and effect modification, we need to undertake **multivariable analysis**.

As you know, the type of regression that we should choose depends on the nature of our data. In this case, our event of interest -or dependent variable- is seroconversion (remember that this is when a person has a recorded HIV-negative test followed by a positive one) which is a binary variable. We saw earlier on that logistic regression is the appropriate method when we have this type of dependent variable. This is true. However, in our database we also have the dates for the different tests. Having time-to-event data gives us the possibility to model the **rates of seroconversion**. The incidence rate (or incidence density) is the number of incident cases divided by the person-time at risk. In this specific sentinel surveillance system, the numerator is the number of seroconverters, and the denominator is the total person-time at risk, i.e.:

-   the time between the first HIV test (by definition negative in this database) and the first HIV positive test for seroconverters, OR

-   the time between the first HIV test and the last HIV negative test for non-seroconverters).


A regression method that allows to take into account **varying exposure times** to **model incidence rates** is **Poisson regression**, which we will use in this case. 

Often, more than one method is appropriate for the same data. In this case, a Cox Proportional Hazards Regression would also be appropriate. We will cover this method in a separate case study.


#### 4.1: Data preparation for poisson regression

In order to take time-to-event information into account we first need to create a variable with the time at risk for each observation. Also, currently, our dependent variable *seroco* is of `class()` factor, but given that Poisson regression is defined for count outcomes, we need to transform it into numeric.

**Tasks**

-   Create a variable named *seroco_num* which is the numeric version of *seroco*.

-   Create a variable named *timeatrisk* with the number of years at risk for each observation in the dataset


<details>

<summary style="text-decoration: underline; color: darkgreen;">

`r fontawesome::fa("lightbulb", fill = "gold")` Click to read a hint

</summary>

</br>

You should be able to transform *seroco* into numeric without any issues, but if you struggle to remember how, have a look at the EpiRhandbook section on [Converting column class](https://epirhandbook.com/en/new_pages/cleaning.html#convert-column-class).
The time at risk will depend on whether the person seroconverted or didn't. For those who seroconverted we can calculate the number of days at risk as the difference between the first positive test (*first_pos*) and the first test they carried out (*first_test*). For those who did not serconvert the number of days at risk is the difference between the last negative test (*last_neg*) and the first test they carried out (*first_test*).  In order to transform this substraction into a number we can place it inside the function `as.double()`. The result will be the number of days at risk. If we want to transform this into years at risk, we can divide it by 365.25. For more information have a look at the EpiRhandbook section on [Observation time](https://epirhandbook.com/en/new_pages/survey_analysis.html#observation-time)

</br>

</details>



<details>

<summary style="text-decoration: underline; color: red;">

`r fontawesome::fa("check", fill = "red")`Click to see a solution (try
it yourself first!)

</summary>

</br>

```{r , echo=TRUE}
# The outcome variable needs to be a numeric variable for this part of the analysis.
# (because Poisson regression is defined for a count outcome.)
# Therefore, we transform 'seroco' as follows:
hiv.data <- hiv.data %>% 
  mutate(seroco_num = if_else(seroco == "yes", 1, 0))


# Furthermore, the time at risk (before seroconversion) differs between individuals;
# this will be incorporated into the model by an adjustment/denominator term.
# We calculate the denominator ('timeatrisk') for seroco == 0 and seroco == 1:
hiv.data <- hiv.data %>%
  mutate(timeatrisk = as.numeric(if_else
                                 (seroco == "yes", 
                                   (first_pos-first_test)/365.25,
                                   (last_neg-first_test)/365.25)))
```

</br>

</details>


#### 4.2: Fit the poisson regression model with the variable *idu* only

Let’s start fitting the model with one variable only (variable *idu*)

**Tasks**

-   Create a model using poisson regression in which the outcome variable is the column *seroco_num* and the offset is the logarithm of the column *timeatrisk*

-   Interpret the results


<details>

<summary style="text-decoration: underline; color: darkgreen;">

`r fontawesome::fa("lightbulb", fill = "gold")` Click to read a hint

</summary>

</br>

As we did with logistic regression, we can use the ‘generalized linear models’ or `glm()` function to perform poisson regression regression in R. Its basic syntax is: `glm([OUTCOME] ~ [COVARIATES])`. glm stands for ‘generalized linear models’ and can handle a range of different regression models. 
There are two different aspects in the poisson regression formula with respect to the logistic regression one. **The first is the `family =` argument**. In Poisson regression we should write this argument as: 

`glm([OUTCOME] ~ [PREDICTOR], family = poisson(link = "log"))`

By saying that the family is "poisson" we are saying that our outcome is a count and that the counts follow a Poisson distribution, which is appropriate for rare events occurring independently over a fixed period or space.
The 'link = "log"' part means that the expected value of the dependent variable will be transformed using the natural logarithm. This has several important purposes such as ensuring non-negative predictions or ensuring that 
the relationship between the predictors and the log of the expected count is linear, which simplifies interpretation and estimation.

The second thing we need to ensure in Poisson regression is that **we add an `offset = ` argument**. The offset is used to account for varying exposure times among individuals (*timeatrisk*). It adjusts the model to ensure that the **rate** of the event (seroconversion) is correctly estimated, rather than just the count. The offset is typically included on the log scale, which aligns with the log link function used in Poisson regression, so it would look as: `offset = log(timeatrisk)` in our formula.

Finally, to interpret the result you can use the `tidy()` function, which we have used in previous case studies. Ensure that you add the arguments `exponentiate = TRUE` and `conf.int = TRUE` so that the results are transformed from logarithm scale into the orginal scale and they are presented with their confidence interval.


</br>

</details>


<details>

<summary style="text-decoration: underline; color: red;">

`r fontawesome::fa("check", fill = "red")`Click to see a solution (try
it yourself first!)

</summary>

</br>

```{r , echo=TRUE}
#create the model
model1 <- glm(seroco_num ~ idu, #seroconversion as dependent variable and idu as independent variable
              family = poisson(link = "log"), #family for poisson regression
              data = hiv.data, 
              offset = log(timeatrisk)) #offset is the log of our time-varying variable
#get the results
summary(model1) 

#for interpretation, we transform the results back from their log scale and add confidence intervals
tidy(model1, exponentiate = TRUE, conf.int = TRUE)
```

</br>

</details>


