So, the results are also in this case almost identical to the previous two models. The advantage of the quasi-poisson model is that you can adjust for overdispersion without changing the underlying mean structure of the Poisson model. One important disadvantage, however, is that the lack of a true likelihood function in the quasi-Poisson model prevents the calculation of AIC, which relies on the log-likelihood. Thus, you cannot compare model performance with the AIC or the logLik.

### **(Optional) Step 7: Analysis of aggregated data**

Until now, we applied the Poisson regression models on the individual data (each row corresponds to one person). Specifically, since persons were followed up during several (and different) years, we already considered their ‘time at risk’ in order to obtain sensible estimates for incidence rate ratios and incorporated those as offsets into the model.

This approach is pursued usually as an analogue to logistic regression, for obtaining risk ratios or incidence rate ratios (as we did above). Following the fundamental concept of Poisson regression, however, leads to a different perspective where we specify aggregated units for which we observe corresponding counts of events. For instance, we can consider the yearly incidence and apply the Poisson regression model to data aggregated by sex, age2 etc.

To explore this idea, we will define a dataframe based on hiv.data that contains for each combination of year and relevant grouping variables (such as sex and age2) the number of people that are at risk of seroconversion and the number of actually measured seroconversions. 


#### 7.1: Creating an aggregated dataframe

**Task**

-   Convert the case-based data into aggregated format in which each row has the number of serconversions and the time at risk by age, sex, idu (we only include this risk factor to simplify), and years from 1995 until 2004 only. 

<details>

<summary style="text-decoration: underline; color: darkgreen;">

`r fontawesome::fa("lightbulb", fill = "gold")` Click to read a hint

</summary>

</br>

Do not worry if you don't manage. The syntax here is a bit more advanced as it uses logical expressions applied to vectors. It is alright to skip understanding the details of this loop first and come back to it after checking the regression results
 
</br>

</details>



<details>

<summary style="text-decoration: underline; color: red;">

`r fontawesome::fa("check", fill = "red")`Click to see a solution (try it yourself first!)

</summary>

</br>
```{r , echo=TRUE}
# Step 1: Create all combinations of year, sex, age2, and idu
# This generates a data frame with all possible combinations of the variables
A <- expand_grid(
  year = 1989:2004,                # Years of interest
  sex = levels(hiv.data$sex),      # Levels of the 'sex' variable
  age2 = levels(hiv.data$age2),    # Levels of the 'age2' variable
  idu = levels(hiv.data$idu)       # Levels of the 'idu' variable
)

# Step 2: Aggregate data
# For each combination of year, sex, age2, and idu, calculate:
# - 'atrisk': Number of people at risk for seroconversion in that year
# - 'seroconversions': Number of seroconversions in that year
agg_db <- A %>%
  rowwise() %>% # Process each row independently (needed for custom calculations)
  mutate(
    # Calculate the number of people at risk
    atrisk = sum(
      year(hiv.data$first_test) <= year &  # Tested for the first time on or before the current year
        hiv.data$lasttestY <= year &      # Last test year is on or before the current year
        hiv.data$sex == sex &             # Matches the current 'sex' level
        hiv.data$age2 == age2 &           # Matches the current 'age2' level
        hiv.data$idu == idu               # Matches the current 'idu' level
    ) -
      sum(
        hiv.data$sex == sex &               # Matches the current 'sex' level
          hiv.data$age2 == age2 &           # Matches the current 'age2' level
          hiv.data$idu == idu &             # Matches the current 'idu' level
          hiv.data$first_pos <= year + 1,   # First positive test is before or during the next year
        na.rm = TRUE                        # Ignore missing values
      ),
    
    # Calculate the number of seroconversions
    seroconversions = sum(
      year(hiv.data$first_pos) == year &  # First positive test is in the current year
        hiv.data$sex == sex &             # Matches the current 'sex' level
        hiv.data$age2 == age2 &           # Matches the current 'age2' level
        hiv.data$idu == idu,              # Matches the current 'idu' level
      na.rm = TRUE                        # Ignore missing values
    )
  ) %>%
  ungroup()  %>% 
  
  filter(atrisk > 0) # This step removes combinations where 'atrisk' is 0 or less


# Look at the first rows of this new database
head(agg_db)
```

</br>

</details>

